File 00Readme - Readme file of the Matlab implementation of the  
Wedge method for derivative free optimization described in

Wedge Trust Region Methods For Derivative Free Optimization
M. Marazzi and J. Nocedal, Technical Report OTC 2000/10,
Northwestern University, Evanston, Illinois, October 2000.
(To appear in Mathematical Programming.)

Specs file setSpecs.m
--------------------- 
This file sets the specs to its default values. Here we explain a 
few non-immediate variables.

nRunsOptn:
There is certain degree of randomness in the initial set 
of sample points (and hence, in the output). Therefore there will 
be variations in the results. We recommend that each problem be 
run a number of times. The default is to run a problem 5 times, 
if nRunsOptn = 'Var'. If only one run is desired, set nRunsOptn 
= 'One'.

AlgOptn:
To run the linear or the quadratic versions of the algorithm, set 
AlgOptn = 'Linear' or 'Quadra' respectively.

Defining a problem
------------------
The user has to supply the files 

user_ufn.m     defines the objective function
user_unames.m  defines the problem name
user_usetup.m  defines the starting point

(These files are analogous to the ones supplied by the CUTE 
test suite.)

An example of these files is supplied.

To run the algorithm, start a Matlab session, and at the Matlab
prompt say "driver". The output files <problem_name>.out and 
<problem_name>.sum will be generated. The output can also go
to the screen, and this is governed through the variable 
iPrint in the specs file. At the end of this file a sample output
is included.

Fortran subroutine
------------------
The algorithm uses the Fortran subroutine gqtpar.f (by More' and 
Sorensen) from Minpack to solve the trust region subproblem. 
A so-called gateway function (or wrapper) gqtparg.f is supplied 
so that gqtpar.f can be called from Matlab. The user has to generate 
the architecture-dependent mex-file gqtpar.mex* (the extension depends 
on the architecture, e.g. gqtpar.mexsol for Solaris) by saying

fmex gqtpar.f gqtparg.f

at the Unix prompt. The above command may be system dependent. If this
doesn't work, you will have to see your Matlab documentation on mex-files.

